  "why": "The statement asks whether 509,203 is the least Riesel number; a yes needs a prime for every remaining odd k below it, a no needs a covering set (or other proof) for one. The base-2 prime discoveries each eliminate one of finitely many candidates by exhibiting a single prime: these are finite checks that narrow where a smaller Riesel number could hide without settling the question, so I scored them instances, including the only dated in-window one (107347, Aug 2024). The close call was case versus instances for these: one could read each k as a restriction, but a single prime found by search is exactly the finite-certificate pattern F5 describes, and 41 candidates remain. Riesel 1956 establishes the statement's presupposition (509,203 is Riesel) via a hand-checkable cover, scored case/checkable as a witness; it is pre-snapshot and cannot make the problem open_stale. The ACL2 paper formalises a known result (none). Brown's least-exponent computation for 104917, the repdigit paper, FFK's covering-free Riesel numbers, and the base-5 eliminations all answer different questions (variant). No AI system is credited anywhere.",
